cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "video-to-llm"
message: >-
  If you used this to prepare video evidence for published work, please cite it.
  Two of the audiences this was built for — researchers with interview or field
  recordings, and people working with depositions and case material — cite their
  tools, and a tool that produces citable timestamps should be citable itself.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Navdeep
    family-names: Singh
    alias: navdeep-h-singh
repository-code: "https://github.com/navdeep-h-singh/video-to-llm"
url: "https://github.com/navdeep-h-singh/video-to-llm"
abstract: >-
  A localhost-only tool that turns hours of local video into one chronological,
  timestamped document an LLM can read — speech, marked silences, and optionally
  what was on screen, in the order it happened. Every line carries a timestamp
  that resolves back to the exact frame behind it, so a claim a model makes can
  be checked against the picture it came from. Processing happens once and the
  result is reused for every later question. Nothing is uploaded: the interface
  binds loopback, descriptions are off by default, and a job that leaves them
  off makes no network request at all.
keywords:
  - video
  - transcription
  - whisper
  - ffmpeg
  - local-first
  - privacy
  - offline
  - large language models
  - retrieval augmented generation
  - qualitative research
  - provenance
license: MIT
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-08-22"
